In article <2hl0hh$g9a@scunix2.harvard.edu>, galle1@husc7.harvard.edu (Brian Galle) writes:
>1. Enchant armor scrolls enchant a random piece of armor.  Wrong.  They enchant
> the outermost piece (cloak before armor before Hawaiian shirt, for instance.)

Well, sure this is is technically wrong, but really they just meant it picks
one of your exposed pieces of armor at random.

>2. Enchanting a +3 piece of armor will dissolve it.  Again wrong.  The odds
> of detroying armor, unless I'm much mistaken, are 1/(the current bonus of
> the armor).  I'm not sure what happens if the armor has a negative bonus, or
> if what I've said is true for +2 armor (but I think it is).

The code says:
        if((otmp->spe > ((otmp->otyp == ELVEN_MITHRIL_COAT) ? 5 : 3))
             && rn2(otmp->spe) && !sobj->cursed) {
        Your("%s violently %s%s for a while, then evaporates.",
If the enchantment is >3 (>5 for elven mithril), there is an chance it
will evaporate.  The chance is (enchantment-1)/enchantment.   All this 
code is before anything is added to the enchantment, so it is the
current enchantment.  In other words, you can enchant it once more safely
when it's at +3.

Some other poster quoted a 3.0 spoiler saying it could theoretically
be enchanted up to maxint (but unlikely).  This isn't true in 3.1.
Some tricky code later on prevents it from going over 9:

                s = sobj->cursed ? -1 :
                    otmp->spe >= 9 ? (rn2(otmp->spe) == 0) :
                    sobj->blessed ? rnd(3-otmp->spe/3) : 1;



